      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251604",
      ":question/problem": "Consider two paired samples of size $n = 50$ from the random variables $X$ and $Y$. Given that the mean of the differences $d_{i} = x_{i} - y_{i}$ is $d^(\u2015) = 1.1$, and the variance of those differences is $s^{2} = 7^{2}$, we wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that $\u03bc \\ne 0$, where $\u03bc$ is the mean of $D = X - Y$.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n| $k\u2216p$ | $0.005$ | $0.010$ | $0.025$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $49$ | $2.680$ | $2.405$ | $2.010$ |\n| $50$ | $2.678$ | $2.403$ | $2.009$ |\n| $51$ | $2.676$ | $2.402$ | $2.008$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $1%$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $-2.676$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nAt the $1%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-4}}\nevidence that $\u03bc \\ne 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
